The one real risk in this translation is that the C performs out-of-range shifts — [38;5;10mval <<= 64 - bits[0m is undefined when [38;5;10mbits[0m is 0 or above 64 — which a compiler is free to treat differently at different optimization levels. The Rust uses [38;5;10mwrapping_shl[0m/[38;5;10mwrapping_shr[0m, matching the x86-64 shift-count-mod-64 behavior. To confirm that assumption is not fragile, I compiled the C at [38;5;10m-O0[0m, [38;5;10m-O1[0m, [38;5;10m-O2[0m, [38;5;10m-O3[0m, and [38;5;10m-Os[0m (into [38;5;10m/tmp[0m, leaving [38;5;10mc_src/[0m untouched) and ran the full suite against each; all five agree with the Rust. The test reads an optional [38;5;10mC_LIB_PATH[0m environment variable so that comparison can be repeated.[0m[0m
